{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>The fountain of Jacob.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): he who came forth from the fountain of Jacob, and likewise (Isaiah 48:1) ״וממי יהודה יצאו״. And he said אל ארץ as in בארץ, and likewise (I Kings 8:35) ״והתפללו אל המקום הזה״. And the reason he mentioned Jacob in connection with the inheritance of the land is to make clear that the holy land was given to them alone — that is, to the seed of Jacob alone — and this is the expression עין יעקב, for we do not find עין אברהם ויצחק but only עין יעקב, to exclude Esau and Ishmael.\n\nKnow that there are four things in which the nation of Israel merited, and they are: prophecy, the Torah, the land of Israel, and the resurrection of the dead. And it seems to me to prove and to explicate all of them from the words of Moses, who revealed all four; and in each and every verse you will find explicitly that he mentioned there the name Jacob, to indicate that all of them were given only to the seed of Jacob alone.\n\nProphecy: as it is written (Deuteronomy 18:15) ״נביא מקרבך מאחיך כמוני״ — he said כמוני, 'like me,' for I am of the seed of Jacob; and from this we learn that prophecy is found only in the seed of Jacob. For since he mentioned מאחיך, and the children of Esau are called brothers, as it is said (Numbers 20:14) ״כה אמר אחיך ישראל״, therefore he needed to add כמוני to exclude the other brothers, who are Esau and Ishmael, and to remove them from the general category. From this you learn that there is no prophecy except in Israel. And that which we find in Balaam, who was a prophet among the nations — it was a chance occurrence for him, and he did not attain that level of attainment except for the honor of Israel, and only for the moment and by way of happenstance; and thus Scripture used the expression of chance (Numbers 23): ״ויקר אלהים אל בלעם״, ״ויקר ה׳ אל בלעם״. Or for the reason that our Sages, of blessed memory, explained: so that the nations would not have an opening of the mouth on the Day of Judgment to say, 'You had prophets for Israel but no prophets for us; had we had prophets, we would have returned to the good.'\n\nThe Torah: as it is written ״תורה צוה לנו משה״, and immediately he mentioned ״קהלת יעקב״, for the Torah was given only to the congregation of Jacob, and there is none who merits it except Jacob alone and all who assemble with him.\n\nThe land: this is what he mentioned here, ״עין יעקב אל ארץ״ — that the land was not given as an inheritance except to the seed of Jacob; and it was never settled by another people after it was destroyed, nor will it be settled by another people.\n\nThe resurrection of the dead: this is what he said, ״אף שמיו יערפו טל״. The word שמיו refers back to עין יעקב and to the preeminence of the seed of Jacob — He ascribed to him heavens, which are the heights ('aravot) of which he mentioned above, ״רוכב שמים״; and He said ״יערפו טל״, for the dew by which the dead are destined to be revived is in the heights, and the other nations do not merit the resurrection of the dead — only Israel, the seed of Jacob. And thus it is written (Daniel 12:2) ״ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו״, and it is known that the word רבים refers to Israel, in the manner of what is written (Esther 8:17) ״ורבים מעמי הארץ מתיהדים״. Thus our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded in the Sifrei: said Rabbi Simai (Psalms 50:4) ״יקרא אל השמים מעל״ — this is the soul; ״ואל הארץ״ — this is the body; ״לדין עמו״ — to Him who judges; ״לעמו״ — from here is a proof for the resurrection of the dead for Israel. End quote.\n\nAnd likewise we find Isaiah, of blessed memory, who prophesied concerning the nations of the world and said (Isaiah 26:14) ״מתים בל יחיו רפאים בל יקומו״, and he turned and prophesied concerning Israel and said (ibid.) ״יחיו מתיך נבלתי יקומון הקיצו ורננו שוכני עפר כי טל אורות טלך״ — explaining that the resurrection of the dead is only for Israel.\n\nAnd you should know that all Israel will be revived, both the wicked and the righteous: the righteous to receive reward, and the wicked to receive punishment. For since they fulfilled the commandments or transgressed them in body and in soul, they are likewise fit to receive the reward or the punishment in body and in soul. And this is what is written (Daniel 12:2) ״אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם״. And this is what Asaph said (Psalms 73:20) ״כצלם תבזה״; and the word 'image' (צלם) refers to the body. And Targum Jonathan rendered: 'like a dream of a man who awakens on the day of the great judgment — so will He awaken them from their graves, and the likeness of their forms will be despised.' And thus we learned in a Beraita: three groups on the Day of Judgment — one of complete righteous, one of complete wicked, and one of intermediate. Complete righteous are written and sealed immediately for life in the World to Come; complete wicked are written and sealed immediately for Gehenna, as it is said (Daniel 12:2) ״ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו אלה לחיי עולם״ etc. From this you learn that even the wicked are revived, and that revival is for their detriment, for they will be judged in body and in soul in Gehenna, which is fixed in the earth in seven chambers within it. And they said there: the intermediate squeak and go up; but those whose transgressions outnumber their merits — and included among this sin are the wicked of Israel who sinned with their bodies — descend to Gehenna and are judged there twelve months. After twelve months, their bodies are consumed and their souls are burned, and a wind scatters them beneath the soles of the righteous. And the statement that their bodies are consumed proves that they descend there to Gehenna in body and in soul. And in order to hint that the resurrection of the dead is only for Israel, therefore Scripture mentioned טל and immediately joined to it ״אשריך ישראל״. And our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded (Deuteronomy 34:2) ״עד הים האחרון״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses as far as the time of the resurrection of the dead, as it is said ״עד הים האחרון״ — do not read הים האחרון but rather היום האחרון." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> עין יעקב — in the manner of the blessing that Jacob their father blessed them (Genesis 48:21): ״והשיב אתכם אל ארץ אבותיכם״." "<b>Also His heavens shall drop down dew.</b> The pronoun 'His heavens' refers back to the Holy One, blessed be He — and likewise ויגרש and ויאמר. Or the pronoun may refer back to Israel, as one who hangs the lesser matter upon the greater, and he spoke thus for the preeminence of Israel, according to the view of those who say that Israel is the principal of existence. Or it may refer back to the land, for we find the word ארץ in masculine form (Genesis 19:13) ״ולא נשא אותם הארץ״, and likewise (Isaiah 9:18) ״נעתם ארץ״; and the sense of the verse is: the heavens of the land, that is, the heavens that are opposite the holy land, shall drop dew — meaning, they shall bring down dew of blessing. And the word יערפו is like ירעפו."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "Source text of the Psalms 73:20 quotation reads כצלם תבזה; the lemma citation is given as בעיר צלמם תבזה, which appears to be a corrupt conflation. Translated the actual quoted phrase. The citation reference for Asaph's statement corrected to Psalms 73:20; the source's reference appears shifted."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:53.270878146Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}